{"name":"Pixel White","hex":"#dbdcdb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pixel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the tiny dots that make up the representation of an image in a computer's memory.","One of the squares that make up a work of pixel art or a zoomed-in image in a computer."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To create computer graphics by editing individual pixels."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pixel"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pixel","blocks":[{"text":"In digital imaging, a pixel, pel, or picture element is the smallest addressable element of a raster image. In a display device, dot matrix printer, or ASCII art a pixel is the smallest element whose output can generally be controlled independently."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Pixel-example.png/330px-Pixel-example.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pixel","wikidata":"Q355198"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Closeup_of_pixels.JPG/330px-Closeup_of_pixels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Closeup_of_pixels.JPG/330px-Closeup_of_pixels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A photograph of subpixel display elements on a laptop's LCD screen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACloseup_of_pixels.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/ReconstructionsFromPixels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/ReconstructionsFromPixels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A pixel does not need to be rendered as a small square. This image shows alternative ways of reconstructing an image from a set of pixel values, using dots, lines, or smooth filtering.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReconstructionsFromPixels.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Linguagem_Eletr%C3%B4nica.jpg/500px-FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Linguagem_Eletr%C3%B4nica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Linguagem_Eletr%C3%B4nica.jpg/500px-FILE_Hipers%C3%B4nica_Rio_09_-_8_BIT_Game_People%2C_Festival_Internacional_de_Lingu